Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: glink: an endpoint destroyed from its rpmsg device's own remove only decouples the device

Since 7.0, qcom_glink_destroy_ept() unregisters the channel's rpmsg
device (qcom_glink_remove_rpmsg_device()). It is also called from that
very device's remove, which runs with the device lock held, and then
waits for that lock for good:

  unbind_store()                          device lock of the rpmsg device
   device_release_driver_internal()
    rpmsg_dev_remove()
     rpmsg_destroy_ept(rpdev->ept)
      qcom_glink_destroy_ept()
       qcom_glink_remove_rpmsg_device()
        rpmsg_unregister_device()          the same device
         device_unregister()
          device_del()
           device_lock()                   held above: D state, for good

On the Pro1X: unbinding qcom_smd_qrtr from the modem's IPCRTR through
sysfs (KolibriOS's pro1x-kexec.sh -m did, to close the channel before a
kexec)

  echo -n 6080000.remoteproc:glink-edge.IPCRTR.-1.-1 \
      > /sys/bus/rpmsg/drivers/qcom_smd_qrtr/unbind

never returned, and the WLAN firmware crashed with QRTR gone under
ath10k. The shutdown hung on the same device later: no network, no
getty, no crash dump, the phone dead until the power key was held. The
same happens to any glink rpmsg driver unbound or removed that way.

When the endpoint is the rpmsg device's own (ept->rpdev ==
channel->rpdev), the driver core is removing or unbinding that device
and owns it: it is only decoupled from the channel, as before 7.0.
Endpoints of other devices still unregister the channel's device.

diff --git a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
index 401a4ec..7ba282c 100644
--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
@@ -1418,8 +1418,17 @@ static void qcom_glink_destroy_ept(struct rpmsg_endpoint *ept)
 	channel->ept.cb = NULL;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&channel->recv_lock, flags);
 
-	/* Decouple the potential rpdev from the channel */
-	qcom_glink_remove_rpmsg_device(glink, channel);
+	/*
+	 * Decouple the potential rpdev from the channel. When the endpoint is
+	 * the rpdev's own, this runs from that device's remove (an unbind, or
+	 * the driver going away) with its device lock held: unregistering it
+	 * here would wait for that lock in device_del() for good. The driver
+	 * core owns the device then; it is only decoupled.
+	 */
+	if (channel->rpdev && ept->rpdev == channel->rpdev)
+		channel->rpdev = NULL;
+	else
+		qcom_glink_remove_rpmsg_device(glink, channel);
 
 	qcom_glink_send_close_req(glink, channel);
 }
